00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.880
There are so, so, so many microbes in the
world.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.539
This far into our journey, it’s kind of
astonishing to think that for all of the exciting

00:00:36.539 --> 00:00:42.440
and odd creatures we’ve watched, we’ve
only kinda dipped into the shallows

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.580
of the microcosmos.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:57.809
And like, have you ever sat back and been
overwhelmed by the enormity of this minuscule world?

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:04.140
Of just how many organisms are out there,
both known and unknown, whose lives have been

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:11.060
sustaining ecosystems and changing climates
and doing just so much stuff for eons and

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:16.100
eons as this diverse, invisible mass?

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:22.100
Scientists have been parsing through this
overwhelming world for a few centuries now,

00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:27.829
identifying the specifics of how individual
organisms live and connecting them to the

00:01:27.829 --> 00:01:31.740
bigger picture of how our world works.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:37.939
But what we especially love to do with the
natural world is categorize, and perhaps the

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:44.679
most familiar image of the categories we’ve
created is the phylogenetic tree.

00:01:44.679 --> 00:01:51.659
The goal of phylogenetic trees is to track
the organisms we know of through their place

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:52.659
in evolution.

00:01:52.659 --> 00:02:01.420
Take, for example, these various corkscrew-shaped
bacteria, members of the Spirochaetes phylum.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:07.429
This phylogenetic tree is from the 90s and
has been updated with more data, but we’ll

00:02:07.429 --> 00:02:12.550
use it just for now because it's relatively
simple and that’s useful for explaining

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.440
how the trees themselves work.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:22.000
The ends of these trees are the various spirochetes
that scientists have been able to study, and

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.170
if you move to the left, you can see their
branches coming to an unnamed ancestor shared

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.200
with other similar spirochetes.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.890
These ancestors then branch back and connect
with each other in various structures, creating

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:42.140
that oh-so-familiar tree-like structure.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:48.730
And at the bottom is E. Coli, which functions
here as an outgroup, a useful reference as

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:53.380
something not immediately part of the Spirochete
phylum.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:59.220
We use these diagrams along with the words
“kingdom” and “phylum” to categorize

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:05.010
everything from the tiniest microbe to the
largest whale, but it’s important to remember

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:10.340
that these trees are our interpretation of
what we’ve seen.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:17.790
Nature didn’t set out to create kingdoms
and phylums, those are categories we created

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.430
to help us make sense of the natural world.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:26.800
It takes a lot of complex work to simplify
nature in this way, and the process behind

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.110
creating these evolutionary trees has itself
been an evolving one.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:38.060
In the days of Darwin, evolution was connected
to the things we could see, and when it comes

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:45.560
to macroscopic animals, the things we see,
like anatomy and fossils, are very useful.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.990
Microbes though, are a bit tougher.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:53.160
When we first began observing them, we tried
to understand them with words we already knew,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:04:00.080
which led to confusion over whether they should
be considered tiny plants or tiny animals.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.970
But with time and more observations, scientists
began to divide these organisms, and really

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:13.830
all of life, into two overarching categories:
bacteria and eukaryotes.

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:19.320
This dichotomy would be most obvious in the
microcosmos, but these divisions were still

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:25.130
just a container that hid a confusing array
of connections.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:30.570
Bacteria, for example, might be grouped together
according to all sorts of factors, like their

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.150
ability to form colonies or the danger they
posed to humans.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:40.180
But it hadn’t always been clear that the
commonality of these traits necessary links

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.460
in anyway to how they evolve.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:49.010
We’ve seen this again and again in our journey
through the microcosmos: in the amoebas and

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:55.560
euglenoids and so many other organisms whose
evolutionary connections seem straightforward

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.490
based on their morphology.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:01.500
And then, inevitably, we bring out the source
of so many plot twists.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.560
We’re talking about molecular phylogeny
and the host of techniques it encompasses

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:12.600
to reveal genetic connections that our eyes
cannot see.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:17.530
But molecular phylogeny doesn’t just show
us a whole bunch of things we got wrong.

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:23.990
It’s helped reveal new categories and relationships
within the microcosmos.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.960
The seminal work behind these techniques was
published by Carl Woese and George Fox in

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:35.990
1977, and it was rooted in a straightforward
but powerful notion, that the sequences of

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:42.440
molecules inside microbes could help us make
connections that their morphology could not.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:49.780
This wasn’t a new idea: scientists had been
linking amino acid sequences between animals.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:55.020
But the real challenge lay in figuring out
what molecular sequence would actually be

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.370
best to accomplish this goal in microbes.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:06.510
We needed something universal enough to connect
all organisms, but also simple enough to compare

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.810
using the sequencing methods available at
the time.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:17.810
So Woese suggested an organelle that you might
not think of all that often: the ribosome.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:26.280
Across all living cells, ribosomes are the
protein factory, taking in RNA and translating

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:32.780
it into the molecules that make our bodies
and all life work.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:39.670
And the ribosome itself has an interesting
structure, made up of both protein and RNA.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:46.970
Woese and Fox picked out a particular subset
of that ribosomal RNA, compared the sequence

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:53.130
different organisms used to construct that
segment, and then built a phylogenetic tree

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:58.310
out of the similarities and differences in
that sequence.

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:05.490
Their results did more than just create a
tree though, it revealed a whole new subset

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:14.699
of life that lay outside what we had assumed to be the binary divide between bacteria and eukaryotes.

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:21.080
They had uncovered the archaea, a type of
prokaryote that had been assumed, up to that

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:26.330
point, to be a strange type of extremophile
bacteria.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:31.940
But thanks to archea’s ribosomal RNA and
additional understanding of their morphology,

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:37.260
Woese and Fox realized that they were separate
from bacteria.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:42.220
An entire new domain of life.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:48.169
Molecular phylogenetic techniques have gone
on to shift our understanding of microbes,

00:07:48.169 --> 00:07:55.510
raising questions and providing insights into
how everything from sex to eating have evolved.

00:07:55.510 --> 00:08:00.780
But there are still many other methods of
evolution that may not be captured by these

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:07.940
techniques, including of course endosymbiogenesis
and horizontal gene transfer.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:12.880
Everybody’s two favorite non-linear methods
of evolution.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:18.580
Establishing these phylogenetic trees, for
all that it has helped us simplify nature,

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:25.820
also emphasizes just how many complexities
we cannot easily illustrate.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:31.310
Connecting organisms through their evolutionary
relationships helps us establish a history

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:37.069
of nature and map out the microbial world
as it exists today.

00:08:37.069 --> 00:08:44.089
That map will likely never be complete, but
the work that goes into it helps us navigate

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:52.589
through time and space and through invisible
lives, long gone, to see how our world today

00:08:52.589 --> 00:08:57.430
is connected to the microcosmos of the past.

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:04.009
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

